## Scope and method

Read-only static review of the working tree for the eleven files the Opus log names, against `datom.world.md`, `dao.stream.md`, `dao.stream.ws.md`, both implementation plans, the prior Fable sign-off, and the committed `dao.stream.ws`, `serving`, `rpc`, and `yin.repl.core` / `v2-adapter` code the fixes compose over. No shell was available, so the unstaged delta was reconstructed from the current file contents plus the Opus log rather than from `git diff`. No tests were run; the reported test evidence was assessed as claims and checked against what each new test actually exercises.

## Findings

Severity | file:line | invariant / evidence | recommended correction
---|---|---|---
High | `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:171-187`, `:221-224` | Explicit control flow / R2 deliverable. The JVM poller no longer exits the process: `poll-loop!` returns after `drain-server!`, and the only `.halt` is on the main thread after `read-loop!`, which is parked in `read-line` and recurs until EOF. A typed `(quit)` prints `Bye`, stops the endpoint, then the JVM stays alive until Ctrl-D. The prior staged code halted from the poller; the delta moved the exit and lost it for the interactive path. No test drives a host loop, so this passes the reported suite. | Halt from the step owner: after `(drain-server! server')` in `poll-loop!`, call `(.halt (Runtime/getRuntime) 0)`, or interrupt/close stdin. Keep the main-thread join only as the headless wait.
Medium | `src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:258-267` with `connect.cljc:386-390` | Operator must not be trapped by control commands (plan, *The REPL driver*). The new "Still disconnecting" branch fires whenever `:detached-by :operator` and the RPC terminal is not `/detached`. After a terminal of `/ended`, `/not-found`, or `/transport-error` (e.g. `(connect …)` to a wrong port, then `(disconnect)` to get local back), every later `(connect …)`, same URL or new, answers "Still disconnecting … once the boundary reports it" forever; the boundary already reported and never will again. Before the delta this path reached `open-connection`. `close!` also overwrites the terminal status with `:closing`, so `(repl-state)` cannot show it. | Gate the "still disconnecting" answer on the terminal not yet having landed (`(nil? (remote-terminal state))`) rather than on non-reattachability; when the terminal is non-reattachable, fall through to `open-connection`. Have `connect/close!` preserve a terminal `:status`. Add a test: transport-error, disconnect, connect again composes a new boundary.
Medium | `src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:190-212`, `:222-231`, `:278-298`; `rpc.cljc:238-244`, `:438-449` | Finding 1 asks that operator disconnect retain no retrying work. The delta clears the driver's `:retrying` shadow but the RPC client's `:unsent` (the retrying request itself) survives `close!`, the terminal event, and `rebind`. After reattach, the next ordinary line calls `submit-remote` → `rpc/request!`, which resends the stale unsent request and ignores the new line; the driver records the stale id as `:outstanding` and the operator's line is silently dropped. Reachable by typing a line before `Connected to …`, then `(disconnect)`, then `(connect …)`. The same clobber exists on the uninvited-drop path because `reattach` clears `:retrying` while `:unsent` persists. Not covered by the new test, whose pre-disconnect line was already outstanding. | On operator disconnect and on reattach, abandon `:unsent` explicitly (clear it and append a completion with a reason so the loss is reported, not silent). In `handle-line`, queue when the RPC state has `:unsent` rather than trusting the driver's `:retrying` shadow, so `retry-unsent` is the only path that resends.
Medium | `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:590-604` with `v2.cljc:157-169`, `:260-263`, `:347-350` | Honest lifecycle. `stop!` marks any non-stopping endpoint `:stopping`, including one with `:status :failed` and no `:serving` (every current build, since `host/websocket` is nil). `step` returns after `drain-lifecycle` for such an endpoint, so `:stopped` never arrives and all three hosts step the full budget (about 5 s) and then print "the endpoint did not report :stopped" for an endpoint that never bound. `port-composes-an-endpoint-that-reports-its-own-bind` stops before quit, so this is untested. | Make `stop!` a no-op (or mark `:stopped` directly) when `(:serving endpoint)` is nil, and have hosts drain only when status is `:starting`, `:running`, or `:stopping`.
Low | `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:107-113`, `:216-219`, `:223` | Off-by-one budgets. The drain performs `stop-ticks + 1` steps with a sleep between each, so it needs more than `tick-millis × stop-ticks`; both JVM joins use exactly that. The join can expire before the drain prints its timeout line, and the JVM then halts (or, in the headless hook, finishes shutdown) mid-drain. Node and Dart count the same 201 ticks but exit from inside the ticker, so only the JVM is affected. | Derive the join budget from the drain: `(* tick-millis (+ stop-ticks 2))` plus slack, or have the poller halt itself (see High) so no join budget is needed.
Low | `src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:180-188`, `:327-335` | After a non-reattachable terminal (`/ended`, `/not-found`) ordinary input is still queued "until (connect …) reattaches", although the notice for those terminals says there is nothing to reattach to and `/not-found` is not retried. Pre-existing, but the new `remote-routed?` predicate is the right place to fix it. | Return to local routing when the terminal is `/ended` or `/not-found` (and the reader-terminal outcomes); keep queueing only for `/detached` and arguably `/transport-error`.
Low | `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:271-277` | Host-seam contract. `accept!` keeps a 2-arity that passes `now` as nil, which silently reproduces finding 3 for any host glue that calls it; `host.cljc:66` documents only the 3-arity. | Drop the 2-arity, or make it read a composition-supplied clock.
Low | `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:458-465` | Vocabulary ownership. An incomplete form is answered with `:dao.stream.apply/malformed-request`, the envelope owner's code for a malformed envelope; this envelope was well-formed and the refusal is an application decision. A client cannot tell a codec defect from an unbalanced form. | Use a serve-owned code such as `:yin.repl.serve/incomplete-input`. Behaviour otherwise correct: the whole unbalanced input is retained before evaluation, so nothing partially evaluates before the reset.
Low | `src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs:220-224` | Fragile callback contract. `ws` selects the async `verifyClient` form by `Function.length === 2`; a variadic or multi-arity cljs function would silently switch to the sync form, whose refusal is 401 with the boolean return ignored. Correct as written. | Comment the arity requirement at the definition; plan the `upgrade`-event handler for the next `ws` major, as the Opus log notes.
Low | `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:213-223`, `:374-385` | Signal lifecycle. Only headless installs a signal trigger. Interactive JVM and Dart with `--port` exit on Ctrl-C with the endpoint live, so clients observe `:ws/closed` instead of `:ws/ended`. Node interactive is covered by readline closing on Ctrl-C. Within the prior finding's stated scope, so deferred rather than a defect. | Install the same trigger in interactive mode, or record the limit in `yin.repl.md` at R5.
